.TH TM 1 "2026-09-07" "tm 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tm \- interactive terminal UI for managing tmux sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tm
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.br
.B tm
.IR NAME \ |\  DIR
.br
.B tm ls
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tm kill
.I NAME
.br
.B tm agents
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tm status
.RB [ \-\-ascii ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tm
replaces the usual
.B tmux ls
\(->
.B tmux attach\fR/\fBtmux new
workflow with a keyboard-driven session manager. It lists all tmux sessions
with window count, number of attached clients, last activity and the
working directory of the active pane, shows a live preview of the bottom of
the highlighted session's active pane, and lets you attach, create, kill,
rename and detach sessions with single keystrokes. Inside tmux the current
session is marked with a triangle.
.PP
.B tm
detects where it runs: outside tmux, selecting a session attaches to it;
inside tmux (when
.B $TMUX
is set), it switches the current client instead, so there is no need to
detach before changing sessions: run
.B tm
from any pane, or bind it to a popup key (see EXAMPLES). A hint line above
the footer names the popup key when one is bound, and shows the binding to
add otherwise. If no tmux server is running, an empty state is shown and
creating a session starts the server.
.PP
Given a
.I NAME
or
.IR DIR ,
.B tm
skips the UI and attaches directly, creating the session first if needed.
.PP
.B tm
also shows which coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Antigravity
and others) run in which tmux pane, and whether each one is working, waiting
for you, or idle; see AGENT STATUS.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show usage and key bindings, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, then exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "tm " NAME
Attach to (or, inside tmux, switch to) session
.IR NAME .
If it does not exist it is created in
.BR sessions.default_dir .
.TP
.BI "tm " DIR
The same for the project in directory
.IR DIR :
the session is named after the directory and started there.
.I DIR
must contain a slash or be
.BR . ", " ..
or start with
.BR ~ ,
so
.B tm .
opens a session for the current directory.
.TP
.BR "tm ls" " [" \-\-json ]
List sessions with window count, attached clients, last activity and the
active pane's working directory. A header row is only printed when standard
output is a terminal;
.B \-\-json
prints a JSON array for scripts, where every session also has an
.B agents
array with the objects described under
.BR "tm agents" .
.TP
.BI "tm kill " NAME
Kill session
.IR NAME .
.TP
.BR "tm agents" " [" \-\-json ]
List the coding agents in all panes, blocked ones first: state, agent,
target
.RI ( session : window . pane ),
pane id, time since the pane last changed, and working directory.
.B \-\-json
prints an array of objects with the keys
.BR session ", " window ", " window_name ", " pane ", " pane_id ", " agent ,
.B state
.RB ( blocked ", " working " or " idle ),
.BR path ", " title
and
.BR seconds_since_change .
.TP
.BR "tm status" " [" \-\-ascii ]
Print one short line for the tmux status bar: a red, green and white circle
emoji, each followed by the number of blocked, working and idle agents
(states without agents are left out); nothing when no agent runs.
.B \-\-ascii
uses letters instead
.RB ( "B1 W2 I1" ).
.PP
.BR "tm agents" ", " "tm status"
and
.B tm ls \-\-json
look at every agent pane twice, 0.7 seconds apart, to see whether it is
changing; they take about a second when agents run.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.SS Session list
.TP
.BR j / k ", arrow keys"
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B Enter
Attach to the selected session (outside tmux) or switch the current client
to it (inside tmux).
.TP
.B n
Create a new session; prompts for a name and an optional start directory.
.TP
.B d
Kill the selected session, after confirmation. An extra warning is shown
when killing the session you are currently inside.
.TP
.B r
Rename the selected session.
.TP
.B D
Detach all clients from the selected session.
.TP
.B p
Open the project sessionizer (see below).
.TP
.B a
Open the agents screen (see below).
.TP
.B /
Filter the session list while typing.
.B Escape
clears the filter,
.B Enter
jumps to the highlighted match.
.TP
.B s
Toggle sorting between session name and last activity.
.TP
.B v
Show or hide the preview pane. The preview is always hidden when the
terminal is narrower than 80 columns.
.TP
.B ?
Show the key help.
.TP
.BR q ", " Escape
Quit.
.SS Project sessionizer
Lists the subdirectories of the configured project roots (default:
.IR ~/git\-projects ).
Typing fuzzy-filters the list, best match first;
.B Enter
selects the highlighted project, arrow keys or
.BR ctrl+n / ctrl+p
move the cursor, and
.B Escape
clears the filter or, when it is empty, returns to the session list.
Selecting a project creates a session named after the directory, started in
that directory \(em or attaches to it if it already exists (marked with a
bullet). Characters that tmux forbids in session names
.RB ( . " and " : )
are replaced with underscores.
.SS Agents screen
One row per pane that runs a coding agent: state, agent, session, window
.RI ( window . pane
when the window is split), working directory and time since the pane last
changed, blocked agents first, then working, then idle. The preview shows
the highlighted agent's pane.
.B Enter
selects that agent's window and pane and attaches or switches to it;
.B /
filters the list;
.B Escape
clears the filter or returns to the session list;
.B ?
shows the key help. The session list shows the same states rolled up per
session in its
.B Agents
column.
.SS Dialogs
.TP
.BR y / n
Confirm or decline kill and detach confirmations.
.TP
.B Escape
Cancel any dialog.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Optional configuration is read from
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux-manager/config.toml
(default:
.IR ~/.config/tmux-manager/config.toml ).
All keys are optional; the defaults are:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[projects]
roots = ["~/git-projects"]

[sessions]
default_dir = "~"

[ui]
list_refresh_seconds = 2.0
preview_refresh_seconds = 2.0
show_preview = true
preview_width = 40
sort = "name"

[agents]
working_grace_seconds = 3.0
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B projects.roots
Directories whose subdirectories appear in the sessionizer.
.TP
.B sessions.default_dir
Pre-filled start directory for new sessions.
.TP
.BR ui.list_refresh_seconds ", " ui.preview_refresh_seconds
Refresh intervals for the session list and the preview pane, in seconds
(minimum 0.2).
.TP
.B ui.show_preview
Whether the preview pane is shown at start (default: true).
.TP
.B ui.preview_width
Width of the preview pane in percent of the terminal width (10\-90,
default: 40).
.TP
.B ui.sort
Initial sort order of the session list:
.B name
or
.B activity
(most recent first).
.TP
.B agents.working_grace_seconds
An agent whose pane changed within this many seconds counts as working
(minimum 0.5, default: 3).
.PP
A config file that cannot be parsed, or a key with the wrong type or an
out-of-range value, is reported with the offending key and
.B tm
exits with status 2.
.SH AGENT STATUS
.B tm
finds agents by reading the process table: it walks the processes below
each pane and matches their names (and the script run by
.BR node ", " bun " or " python )
against the known agents. The command tmux reports for a pane is not
enough; Claude Code, for example, shows up as its version number.
Detected agents: Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Antigravity
.RB ( agy ),
Gemini, Cursor, Copilot, Amp, Cline, Devin, Droid, Grok, Hermes, Kilo, Kimi,
Kiro, Letta, Maki, Muse, Pi, Qoder, Qwen, omp and Mastra Code.
.PP
Each agent pane is in one of three states:
.TP
.B blocked
The agent waits for you: a permission prompt, a question, a trust dialog.
.TP
.B working
The pane's text changed within
.B agents.working_grace_seconds
(for a window with a single pane, tmux's window activity counts too), or
the screen shows a working indicator.
.TP
.B idle
Anything else, usually waiting for the next prompt.
.PP
Two signals decide: activity works for every agent, even one
.B tm
has no rules for; screen rules decide
.BR blocked .
The screen rules are the agent manifests of herdr
(https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr, Apache License 2.0), bundled unchanged
and evaluated by a Python port of herdr's rule engine. An agent whose user
interface changed, or one without a manifest, shows
.B idle
instead of
.B blocked
until the manifest knows the new prompt.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMUX
When set,
.B tm
assumes it runs inside tmux and switches clients instead of attaching.
.TP
.B TM_SOCKET
Route all tmux commands to an isolated server
.RB ( "tmux \-L <socket>" ).
Mainly useful for testing
.B tm
without touching your real sessions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including quitting the UI without selecting a session.
.TP
.B 1
tmux is not installed, or a tmux command failed.
.TP
.B 2
The configuration file is invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tmux-manager/config.toml
Optional configuration file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the manager:
.PP
.RS
$ tm
.RE
.PP
Open a session for the current directory, or jump back into it:
.PP
.RS
$ tm .
.RE
.PP
Feed session names into another tool:
.PP
.RS
$ tm ls \-\-json | jq \-r '.[].name'
.RE
.PP
Show agent status next to the clock in the tmux status bar:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set \-g status\-interval 5
set \-g status\-right '#(tm status) %H:%M'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bind a key in
.I ~/.tmux.conf
to open
.B tm
as a floating popup over the current session:
.PP
.RS
bind S display-popup \-E \-w 80% \-h 75% tm
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Eugen Hinz (https://github.com/cloudnutzer/tm)
